<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.5.1: Numerical Patterns</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01805</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.5.1: Numerical Patterns</md:title>
    <md:uuid>48fa8c50-bced-4fb7-a93f-e2baeb71c5d0</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Warm Up (5 minutes)</title>
<para>The purpose of this warm up is to elicit the idea that the values in the table have a predictable pattern, which will
  be useful when students consider the context of a falling object in a later activity. While students may notice and
  wonder many things about this table, the patterns are the important discussion points, rather than an attempt to find
  a rule for the function. Because the rule is not easy to uncover, studying the numbers ahead of time should prove
  helpful as students analyze the function later.</para>
<para>This prompt gives students opportunities to see and make use of structure. The specific structure they might notice
  is all the \(y\)-values are multiples of 16 and perfect squares. Some may notice the pattern is not linear and wonder
  whether it is quadratic.</para>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Have students write down their thoughts about what they notice and wonder in the table. Then, have them turn and talk
  with a partner. Ask pairs to share their thoughts with the class and add to a growing list. If some of the sample
  answers are not present, then ask questions to facilitate their addition to the class list.</para>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>Study the table. What do you notice? What do you wonder?</para>
<table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
  <thead/>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">
        \(x\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        0
      </entry>
      <entry>
        1
      </entry>
      <entry>
        2
      </entry>
      <entry>
        3
      </entry>
      <entry>
        4
      </entry>
      <entry>
        5
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">
        \(y\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        0
      </entry>
      <entry>
        16
      </entry>
      <entry>
        64
      </entry>
      <entry>
        144
      </entry>
      <entry>
        256
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>400
      </para></entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Answer</para>
<para>Things you may notice:</para>
<list>
  <item> The \(x\)-values increment by 1 as you move across the table. </item>
  <item> The \(y\)-values increase. </item>
  <item> All \(y\)-values are even and divisible by 4, 8, and 16. </item>
  <item> The \(y\)-values are all perfect squares. </item>
  <item> The successive differences between the \(y\)-values are growing. </item>
</list>
<para>Things you may wonder:</para>
<list>
  <item> Is there a rule for these numbers? </item>
  <item> Do the numbers represent the growth of a geometric pattern? </item>
  <item> Do the numbers represent a quadratic relationship? </item>
  <item> What is the next number in the table? </item>
</list>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>Invite students to share their observations and questions. Record and display them for all to see.</para>
<para>After all responses are recorded, tell students that they will investigate these values more closely in upcoming
  activities.</para></section>
</content>
